  "narrative": "The new specificity controls are largely reassuring for the four accepted large associations. H77 tests whether TP53 loss also predicts weaker NFE2L2 dependency in the same way KEAP1 loss does: R54 = +0.016 [-0.025, 0.056] under a positive direction. The point estimate is small and imprecise, with no evidence of a clinically meaningful association. H78 tests whether KEAP1 loss also predicts weaker USP7 dependency: R55 = +0.009 [-0.033, 0.050], also near null. These cross-biomarker nulls support outcome specificity: TP53 loss is associated with USP7 dependency in this dataset, and KEAP1 loss is associated with NFE2L2 dependency, but neither appears to produce the opposite very large cross-outcome effect. This does not exclude small associations, but it argues against the accepted effects being generic manifestations of broad pathway co-alteration.\n\nThe cross-outcome controls are also reassuring. SMARCA4 loss is not meaningfully associated with TMED10 dependency, R56 = -0.005 [-0.025, 0.014], or POLR2A dependency, R57 = -0.005 [-0.024, 0.014]. MSI-high is not meaningfully associated with POLR2A dependency, R58 = -0.004 [-0.035, 0.028]. Because the accepted SMARCA2 and WRN effects are enormous, a generic shift in broad essential dependency or screen-wide quality would plausibly have produced large effects on TMED10 or POLR2A as well. The near-null results therefore support that the SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2 and MSI-high/WRN associations are relatively selective in this dataset.\n\nRole-free marker controls are consistent with specificity. Marker-E is not associated with USP7 or NFE2L2 dependency: R60 = -0.008 [-0.040, 0.025] and R61 = +0.001 [-0.031, 0.033]. Marker-F is also near null for USP7 and NFE2L2: R62 = -0.024 [-0.056, 0.009] and R63 = -0.006 [-0.038, 0.026]. These are far smaller than the accepted large biomarker effects and do not support the idea that assay-agnostic marker structure is driving the main findings. I reject and close these marker-control claims.\n\nThe NF1-loss RAS-pathway explorations are null in the claimed direction. NF1 loss is not associated with stronger RIT1 dependency, R64 = -0.021 [-0.049, 0.008], nor stronger KRAS dependency, R65 = -0.020 [-0.049, 0.009]. These are small and imprecise, and do not support a clinically meaningful dependency-marker association in this dataset. I reject and close them.\n\nThe only new non-null discovery is H80, CDKN2A loss versus MDM2 dependency. Because H80 has direction -1, the signed estimate R59 = +0.117 [0.082, 0.152] corresponds to a negative raw exposed-minus-comparator difference: CDKN2A-loss tumors have more negative MDM2 scores, i.e. stronger MDM2 dependency. The effect is not huge relative to the previously validated associations, which are around +0.8, but it is moderate, precise, and clinically meaningful by the stated threshold: it is about 0.52 standard deviations of MDM2 dependency and its interval excludes very small EGFR-like effects. The direct TP53-loss association with MDM2 was null, so CDKN2A loss may identify a p53-pathway context for MDM2 dependency through a different mechanism, possibly involving cell-cycle or co-deletion structure. Given the large number of discovery comparisons tested, I do not want to close on discovery alone, but the evidence is strong enough to accept the dataset association now while validation is run. I therefore accept H80, keep it active, and request independent validation. If validation confirms, H80 can become an additional robust predictor of stronger MDM2 dependency in this dataset. If validation fails or is unstable, I will revisit its status.\n\nAll conclusions remain dataset-specific associations, not causal claims and not claims of external generalizability.",
  "research_notes": "Post-specificity block. Four very large accepted and validated associations remain: TP53 loss -> weaker USP7 (H11), KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 (H17; H8 rejected), SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 (H7), MSI-high -> stronger WRN (H6). The new analysis block adds a fifth dataset-specific association: H80 CDKN2A loss -> stronger MDM2 dependency, R59 = +0.117 [0.082, 0.152] under direction -1. Accept H80 as a discovery association, keep active, and request validation of H80 because it is novel, clinically meaningful, and discovery-multiplicity-prone. Specificity controls are near null: cross-biomarker H77 TP53/NFE2L2 and H78 KEAP1/USP7; cross-outcome H79 SMARCA4/TMED10, H68 SMARCA4/POLR2A, H76 MSI/POLR2A; marker-E/F controls H42 USP7/E, H47 NFE2L2/E, H74 USP7/F, H75 NFE2L2/F; NF1 exploratory tests H72 RIT1/NF1 and H73 KRAS/NF1. These nulls argue that the four large accepted effects are not generic broad-dependency shifts or role-free-marker artifacts. EGFR amplification remains unresolved and deferred: overall and stratum estimates are small, often imprecise, and not accepted. Next synthesis must assess H80 validation immediately. If H80 validates, consider refinements for co-deletion and p53 context, especially TP53-wildtype, TP53-loss, and MTAP-loss/co-deletion strata; if it does not, downgrade or reject H80 and treat CDKN2A-MDM2 as an underpowered or confounded discovery. No causal or external-generalizability claims.",
